(This is a draft and is incomplete.)

This directory contains the documentation and header files for emulation
of sound cards such as OPL3, and may also contain implementations.

Your plugin must implement the interface defined in musemu.h, and must
define musemu_main, which accepts the arguments:

* flag = The bitwise OR of the MUSEMU_ constants.

* arg = The argument text, which might be an empty string. The pointer is
not valid after this function returns; if you want to keep it, then you
must make a copy of it. (The character encoding is always ASCII; any bytes
outside of the ASCII range are not guaranteed to be any specific character
encoding, but the character encoding is guaranteed to be correct for the
C functions such as fopen, popen, getenv, etc.)

* callback = The callback function. Call this for each emulator that you
are registering. Do not call it after your main function returns.

* cbarg = Pass this as the first argument of the callback function, but
do not do anything else with it, and do not keep it after your main
function returns. After calling the callback function, the pointer to the
MUSEMUinf does not need to be valid, although the OID that it points to
must remain valid and unchanged. The MUSEMU structure must remain valid
and unchanged for the rest of the program, unless the callback returns
a nonzero number (indicating that it is not needed).

It returns a constant error message string if there is an error, or null
if there is no error.

Note that musemu_main may be called more than once with different argument
text in some cases, such as if dlopen is called with the same file name
multiple times. Plugins are not required to support this use, although in
some cases it may be useful e.g. if OIDs can be specified in argument text.

The flags are for musemu_main are:

* MUSEMU_STEREO = Set if the audio output will be stereo, or clear if it
is mono.

* MUSEMU_DEBUG = Set if debug messages should be written to stderr, in
case there is an error, or sometimes for other reasons. The argument text
may contain switches that overrides this.

* MUSEMU_SINGLE_THREAD = Set if it is guaranteed to call all of the
functions in the same thread that the musemu_main function is called in.

* MUSEMU_EXTERNAL_OUT = Means that the host does not expect to receive
audio data from the plugin.

* MUSEMU_FIX_VOLUME = Means that the host will always specify the same
value (usually 1.0) as the values of the ampl and (if stereo) ampr
arguments of the render function, and the render function is allowed to
ignore the value of that argument and treat it as 1.0 instead.

(You can usually safely ignore most of these flags, but it is possible
that some implementations might care about some of them.)

The MUSEMUinf structure contains the OID in binary format, and its size
in bytes. This must be a absolute OID (and does not include the type and
length header; it is only the OID itself).

The MUSEMU structure contains:

* userdata = It can be anything (including null); it will be passed as the
first argument to the "create" function, but not used for anything else.
(The host environment will never dereference this pointer.)

* create = Called to create a new instance of this emulator. It will return
a non-null pointer if it is successful. The return value is used as the
first argument of all of the other functions in this structure; the caller
will never dereference this pointer, but will check if it is null. This
function will not be called until after musemu_main returns.

* destroy = Called to destroy an instance of this emulator; it will be
called exactly once for every created one and then the object will not be
used after it is destroyed.

* reset = Resets an instance of the emulator to the initial state.

* render = Called to render a number of samples. The second argument is
the number of samples (and it will also advance the time by that number
of samples). If outl is not null, the output samples should be multiplied
by ampl and then added to the value already in outl. If outr is not null,
then the same is done with outr and ampr. If it is stereo, then outl is
left and outr is right; if mono, then only outl is used and outr is null.
It is possible for both to be null, in which case it is only time advance.

* poke = Write a value to one of the registers of the emulated sound card.

* peek = Read a value from one of the registers of the emulated sound card.

* load = Load data into the emulated sound card's memory. This is not used
for all kind of sound cards, but some are. It should also be possible to
load data by the DER parameters, in case this function is never called
(Super ZZ Zero never uses this function, but it is provided for the use of
other programs that might use the same API).

* send = Send a (possibly incomplete) MIDI message. (If it is not complete
then the rest of the message will be sent later. Also, it is possible to
include multiple MIDI messages.) (Not all emulators will use MIDI; if they
do not use MIDI then this function will do nothing.)

* extension = Used for extensions beyond this set of functions. The first
argument is the userdata in this structure, and the other arguments specify
which extension, and it returns some kind of pointer relating to that
extension, or null if that extension is not available.

* flag = The flags that are appropriate for this implementation. See the
below list of flags. These flags might be ignored by some programs.

All of the above functions are required (they cannot be null), with the
exception of the "extension" function (which may be null), although it is
not required for any of the functions to do anything.

The "reset", "render", "poke", "peek", "load", and "send" functions may be
called in a separate thread than the main thread, so you should avoid using
any functions that are not thread-safe. The "load" function might be called
in the main thread and in the separate thread. The "reset", "create", and
"destroy" functions are only called in the main thread, and only while the
other thread (if any) is not running.

Arguments of "create" function:

* userdata = Copy of the userdata in the MUSEMU structure.

* samrate = The intended output sample rate. (This might not match the
actual output sample rate if the emulated sound card is overclocked.)

* parameters = DER encoding of zero or more items which are the parameters
for the emulator. This pointer is not valid after this function returns.

* size = The size of the parameters, in bytes. If the size is zero, then
there are no parameters, and the pointer for the parameters might or might
not be a null pointer.

The usage of the parameters, and of peek/poke, is defined by the OID, but
the argument text for musemu_main is by the specific implementation.

If the implementation uses real sound cards, then render might do nothing
other than timing synchronization, and create and destroy might only keep
a counter for the maximum number of instances.

The flags in the MUSEMU structure are:

* MUSEMU_STEREO = Set if this uses stereo output or clear for mono output.
If this is not the same as the mono/stereo flag given to musemu_main, then
it is implementation-dependent whether the render function is called with
the mono or stereo format.

* MUSEMU_EXTERNAL_OUT = Indicates that it does not produce audio data as
output, and instead will sent to something external, such as a real OPL3
sound card, or an external MIDI synthesizer, or a file, etc.

* MUSEMU_EXTERNAL_TIME = Means that external timing is used, so that the
render function should be called at the correct rate and that it should
not be subject to significant latency. (It is unspecified what happens if
this criteria is not met.)

* MUSEMU_FIX_VOLUME = The volume is fixed and cannot be changed (except
possibly by something external to the program). (This can be used if it
controls a sound card that lacks a volume control.)

* MUSEMU_HARD_REAL_TIME = The meaning of this flag is like the LADSPA flag
called LADSPA_PROPERTY_HARD_RT_CAPABLE. See ladspa.h for details.

(Super ZZ Zero does not use these flags at all, but it is possible that
some other programs might have a use for some of them.)
